    title: "Japan cabinet approves record defence budget amid regional tensions",
    slug: 'japan-cabinet-approves-defence-spending-rise',
    category: 'politics',
    cover_image: img('1555848962-6e79363ec58f'),
    created_at: '2025-06-16T09:00:00Z',
    content:
      "<p>TOKYO — Japan's cabinet approved a record defence budget on Monday, pressing ahead with a multi-year plan to raise military spending and acquire longer-range missiles as regional tensions sharpen and allies press for a bigger contribution.</p><p>The package, worth about 8.9 trillion yen, funds new naval vessels, air-defence systems and a growing stockpile of stand-off weapons. It is the third consecutive annual increase under a strategy that aims to lift defence spending toward 2 percent of economic output.</p><p>\"The security environment around us is the most severe since the end of the war,\" the defence minister told reporters. \"We are not seeking confrontation. We are seeking the ability to deter it, so that no one miscalculates and no one misreads our resolve.\"</p><p>The plan relies on higher tax revenue and borrowing. Opponents in parliament questioned whether the spending is sustainable given an ageing population, a shrinking workforce and rising social-welfare costs that already dominate the budget and will only grow.</p><p>\"Every yen for a missile is a yen not spent on childcare,\" one opposition lawmaker said during a committee session. The government disputes that framing, arguing that security underpins economic stability and that a stronger defence attracts investment rather than deterring it.</p><p>Neighbouring governments reacted cautiously. Officials in Beijing and Seoul said they would monitor the build-up, while analysts noted that Japan's alliance with the United States remains the anchor of its strategy and the source of its most advanced equipment and intelligence.</p><p>Defence contractors welcomed the budget. Shares in several shipbuilders and electronics firms rose after the announcement, reflecting expectations of long-term orders and a steadier pipeline of work for domestic suppliers that have struggled with lumpy demand for years.</p><p>Recruitment is a growing concern. The self-defence forces have missed enlistment targets in recent years, as young people choose private-sector jobs with better pay and less disruption. Officials are considering higher salaries, better housing and more flexible career paths.</p><p>Industrial capacity is another constraint. Building advanced missiles and radars requires specialised suppliers, and many have shrunk over decades of low defence spending. Rebuilding that base will take time and steady orders, not one-off surges.</p><p>The budget also funds research into new technologies, including unmanned systems, cyber defences and space-based surveillance. Officials say future conflicts will be decided as much by software and sensors as by ships and aircraft, and they want to avoid falling behind.</p><p>Public opinion is mixed. Many voters accept the need for stronger defences given the regional environment, but they are uneasy about the cost and about the pace of change. \"We want to be safe, but we also want to know the money is well spent,\" one retiree said.</p><p>Diplomats say the budget will be watched closely abroad. Allies will welcome a larger Japanese contribution; neighbours will weigh whether the build-up is defensive, as Tokyo insists, or a signal of a more assertive posture that could unsettle the region.</p><p>Whatever the reading, the fiscal arithmetic is unforgiving. Japan carries the largest public debt in the developed world, and every new commitment competes with the demands of an ageing society. That tension will shape the debate for years.</p><p>\"Money is necessary but not sufficient,\" said a security specialist at a Tokyo think tank. \"Recruitment, industrial capacity and training will decide whether this budget translates into capability. On all three, Japan still has work to do, and the clock is running.\"</p>",
